  "note": "Orbit Data Messages. These are ASCII files rather than octet strings, so the two worked examples annex G prints are shipped as readable .kvn files under corpus and also carried here as decode vectors, hex-encoded because that is the only form the schema has for an input. A consumer can use either. What the decode vectors assert is the text and integer content: the version, the originator, the object identifiers, the frames, the epoch and the number of maneuvers. The numeric state vector is not asserted here, because a vector field has no float accessor and pinning floats as formatted strings would test this package's chosen number formatting rather than the standard. Those values are checked in pkg/odm against the same published text. All four messages are covered. The OCM vectors are mostly about shape, because an OCM's sections are what say how to read its numbers: TRAJ_TYPE names a trajectory row's columns, COV_ORDERING says how a covariance row folds back into a matrix, and MAN_COMPOSITION names a manoeuvre row's fields. They also assert the defaults clause 6.2.1.3 lets a producer leave out, since a keyword that is absent is not a keyword with no value. One OCM vector is in the XML form of clause 8.11 rather than key-value notation, and reports the same fields, so the two forms are held to saying the same thing. The OEM vectors carry the record and block counts as well, because an OEM's shape — how many metadata groups, how many rows, whether a row has acceleration, how many covariance matrices — is the part of it a consumer has to get right before any number matters.",
